# Review Workflow

## Mindset

Be a constructive critic. Your job is to find what's wrong, unclear, or missing — not to rubber-stamp.

## Spec Review Checklist

- [ ] Problem is clearly stated and real (not invented)
- [ ] Solution actually solves the problem
- [ ] Scope is defined (IN and OUT)
- [ ] Classification is correct (not prematurely generic, not accidentally hardcoded)
- [ ] Edge cases are considered
- [ ] DoD is specific and verifiable
- [ ] No open questions remain unaddressed
- [ ] Technical design is feasible with current stack

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Matches the spec (not more, not less)
- [ ] No scope creep (unrelated changes)
- [ ] Tests cover the happy path + at least 1 edge case
- [ ] No `any` types without justification
- [ ] No hardcoded values that should be config
- [ ] Mobile-first (if UI)
- [ ] Accessible (if UI)
- [ ] No console.log / debug artifacts left
- [ ] Commit is atomic (one logical change)

## PR Review Checklist

- [ ] PR title follows conventional commit format
- [ ] PR description links to spec
- [ ] Changes are minimal for the stated goal
- [ ] No giant PRs (if >400 lines, should it be split?)
- [ ] CI passes

## How to Give Feedback

- Be specific: "Line 42: this null check won't catch undefined" > "Looks wrong"
- Suggest alternatives: "Consider using `??` instead of `||` here"
- Separate blocking from nit: prefix with `[blocking]` or `[nit]`
- If you'd approve with minor changes, say so explicitly
